1. What is a GitHub runner?

A GitHub runner is a machine (virtual or physical) that executes workflows defined in GitHub Actions.

It runs jobs like:

Building code

Running tests

Deploying applications

Each job in a workflow is picked up and executed by a runner

👉 Think of it as the execution engine behind your CI/CD pipeline.

2. How do self-hosted runners differ from GitHub-hosted runners?

Feature	                                 GitHub-hosted runners	                                Self-hosted runners

Infrastructure	                           Managed by GitHub	                                     Managed by you
Setup	                                        Ready-to-use	                                You must install & configure
Scalability	                                   Auto-scaled	                                   Manual / custom scaling
Cost	                                    Pay-per-use (free tier available)	                   Your infrastructure cost
Customization	                                  Limited	                                  Full control (OS, tools, network)
Security control	                           GitHub-managed	                                   Fully your responsibility

👉 In short:

GitHub-hosted = convenience

Self-hosted = control + flexibility

3. What security considerations should you take with self-hosted runners?

When using self-hosted runners, you must secure them yourself:

Access Control

Restrict which repositories can use the runner
Use least-privilege principles

Secrets Management

Avoid exposing secrets in logs
Use encrypted secrets from GitHub

Isolation

Use separate runners for sensitive workloads
Avoid sharing runners across untrusted repos

Network Security

Place runners behind firewalls/VPC
Restrict outbound/inbound traffic

Ephemeral Runners

Prefer short-lived runners that reset after each job

Patch & Updates

Regularly update OS and dependencies

👉 Biggest risk: untrusted code execution on your infrastructure

4. How can you scale self-hosted runners?

Scaling can be done in multiple ways:

Horizontal Scaling

Add more runner machines

Auto-scaling with Cloud

Use tools like:

Kubernetes + autoscaling
Cloud VMs (AWS EC2, Azure VM)

Runner Orchestration

Use solutions like:
Actions Runner Controller (ARC) on Kubernetes

Ephemeral Runners

Spin up a new runner per job and destroy after completion

👉 Best practice:
Use containerized + auto-scaling runners for production workloads.

5. Can a single self-hosted runner be used for multiple repositories? Why or why not?

✅ Yes, it can — but with caution.

Why it works:

A self-hosted runner can be registered at:

Repository level
Organization level
Enterprise level
If registered at org/enterprise level → multiple repos can use it

Why it can be risky:

Security risks

One repo’s workflow can affect another

Resource contention

Jobs may queue or slow down

State leakage

Files/artifacts may persist between jobs

👉 Best Practice:

Use separate or ephemeral runners for:

Sensitive projects
Public repositories

Use shared runners only for trusted environments
